% ------------------------------------------------------------- findings ----
\section*{Summary of findings}

\small
\noindent
Every figure is computed from a complete table: an exact distance for every
legal position, both sides to move. Two rule sets run throughout, ordinary
chess and the \emph{capture rule} of \S\ref{sec:rules}; diagrams are badged
\fbox{\scriptsize N} or \fbox{\scriptsize C} accordingly, and where there is a
line to follow the controls under the board step a ply at a time.

\smallskip
\noindent\textbf{Depth obeys a law, except when it does not.}\ Deepest KQK is
mate in $\lceil 3(n-2)/2\rceil$ for every $9\le n\le 60$, $n=8$ the sole
exception. KRK has no such expression, but a recurrence of period six, and it
fails once, at $n=24$. KBNK is quadratic and zig-zags with the parity of the
board, an odd board's corners being all one colour while the mate needs one the
bishop can reach. Square colour settles KBBK outright: the drawn positions are
exactly the same-coloured ones.\hfill(\S\ref{sub:all-depths},
\S\ref{sub:kbnk-colour})

\smallskip
\noindent\textbf{A queen needs no table.}\ Six rules, a pure function of the
position with no lookup and no dependence on $n$, mate from every legal
position on every board from $4\times4$ to $14\times14$ --- 13\,559\,524 of
them --- at worst $1.79$ times the optimum.\hfill(\S\ref{sub:kqk-rule})

\smallskip
\noindent\textbf{Arm the defender and the win can vanish.}\ KQKR grows 35, 44,
54, 69, 85, 108, 132 moves for $n=8\ldots14$, then \textbf{218} at $n=15$; at
$n=16$ a whole class stops being winnable, the drawn fraction jumping from
$0.13\,\%$ to $26.6\,\%$ while the deepest win \emph{falls}.\hfill(\S\ref{sec:kqkr})

\smallskip
\noindent\textbf{Two black kings are easier to mate than one.}\ Under the
strict mating rule White wins every legal position at every size from 3 to 17,
and the $8\times8$ mate is in \textbf{7} against 10 for the same queen against
one king: the method is a fork, not a corner, and in $56.8\,\%$ of the mates
the white king contributes nothing.\hfill(\S\ref{sec:kqkk})

\smallskip
\noindent\textbf{Play for the kings instead and it becomes a fight.}\ Under the
capture rule the same four men give White $65.9\,\%$ of the placements the
mating rule gives him all of, Black holding a third and winning a few. Two
computed facts carry it: \textbf{two kings hunt down a lone king} from every
placement on every board up to $14\times14$, and K\,+\,Q vs K is won everywhere
and a move faster than ordinary KQK. The first has a \textbf{critical board
size} --- at $15\times15$ the hunt fails, 93\,\% of its wins turn drawn, and
the deepest win drops from 62 moves to 37 and stays there to $n=22$. And the
rules are conservative rather than new: with one king a side they are chess
with a single clause replaced, stalemate becoming a loss except in totally
immobile positions, where a \emph{checkmate} becomes a draw
instead.\hfill(\S\ref{sec:rules})

\smallskip
\noindent\textbf{Take the queen away too, and three kings beat two.}\ White
wins from every white-to-move placement on every board from $3\times3$ to
$11\times11$, $1\,987\,925\,940$ of them at $n=11$. The endgame turns on one
row of the census: three kings against one is won with \emph{either} side to
move, so any capture White can make wins on the spot. It is the only table here
in which material falls on both sides, it contains no zugzwang, and the clause
it rests on expires at $15\times15$.\hfill(\S\ref{sec:kings})

\smallskip
\noindent\textbf{``Two knights cannot mate'' is false as stated, and it costs a
table.}\ K+N+N vs K holds \textbf{120} mates on $8\times8$, $16n-8$ on every
board; what is true is that Black is never in \emph{zugzwang}, the deepest
white win being one ply at every size from 3 to 16. So three knights need a
KNNK table underneath them. Under the capture rule --- for this material
exactly chess with stalemate scored as a loss --- the same two knights go from
616 won placements on $8\times8$ to \textbf{5\,713\,976}, then collapse in
their turn: 51, 61, 75, 91, 111, 151, \textbf{219} plies for $n=8\ldots14$, and
$0.45\,\%$ at $15\times15$.\hfill(\S\ref{sec:knights})

\smallskip
\noindent\textbf{What the cross-checks were for.}\ Three of the bugs they
caught were plausible numbers rather than crashes. A successor counter is
\emph{unsound} on a symmetry-reduced index. Treating ``the king steps onto a
piece'' as a capture is sound with one white piece and wrong with two: it cost
KBNK two moves on $8\times8$ and hid in KBBK for as long as KBBK existed. And a
table that \emph{converts} cannot be swept until nothing
changes.\hfill(\S\ref{sec:trust})

\normalsize
\vspace{3mm}
\needspace{8\baselineskip}
