\tableofcontents

\section{The endgames, and what the tables hold}\label{sec:endgames}

One section per holding, each asking the same two questions: what does ordinary
chess make of it, and what does \S\ref{sec:rules}'s capture rule? Every table
is complete. The first four leave Black a lone king and White one or two
long-range men, and every design decision here was made for them; the knights
of \S\ref{sec:knights} leave Black just as bare but break those decisions from
\emph{White's} side; the last three do not leave Black bare at all ---
\S\ref{sec:kqkr} gives him a rook, \S\ref{sec:kqkk} a second \emph{king}, and
\S\ref{sec:kings} takes the pieces away entirely, leaving five kings and the
capture rule as the only game there is.

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrr@{}}
\toprule
& \textbf{White has} & \textbf{$8\times8$ deepest} & \textbf{largest $n$} & \textbf{entries/side there} \\
\midrule
{\color{kqk}$\blacksquare$}~\textbf{KQK}  & king and queen            & mate in 10 & 60 & 5\,820\,822\,000 \\
{\color{krk}$\blacksquare$}~\textbf{KRK}  & king and rook             & mate in 16 & 54 & 3\,092\,041\,836 \\
{\color{kbbk}$\blacksquare$}~\textbf{KBBK}& king and two bishops      & mate in 19 & 22 & 3\,375\,083\,250 \\
{\color{kbnk}$\blacksquare$}~\textbf{KBNK}& king, bishop and knight   & mate in 33 & 19 & 2\,093\,606\,865 \\
\midrule
{\color{kqkr}$\blacksquare$}~\textbf{KQKR}& king and queen \emph{against king and rook}
                                                                      & mate in 35 & 17 & \phantom{0}856\,925\,460 \\
{\color{kqkk}$\blacksquare$}~\textbf{KQKK}& king and queen \emph{against two kings}
                                                                      & mate in \phantom{0}7 & 24 & 6\,667\,930\,368 \\
\midrule
{\color{knnk}$\blacksquare$}~\textbf{KNNK} & king and two knights     & mate in \phantom{0}1 & 16 & \phantom{00}260\,467\,200 \\
{\color{knnnk}$\blacksquare$}~\textbf{KNNNK}& king and three knights  & mate in 21 & 15 & 11\,591\,361\,600 \\
\midrule
{\color{kings}$\blacksquare$}~\textbf{KKKvKK}& three kings \emph{against two} & 63 plies\rlap{$^{\dagger}$} & 11 & 1\,987\,925\,940\rlap{$^{\dagger}$} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}\\[1mm]
{\scriptsize\color{inksoft}$^{\dagger}$~The last holding has no mate to be deep in:
its depth is plies to the capture of the second black king, its limit is the
largest board built rather than the largest that fits, and its last column
counts \emph{placements} rather than the symmetry-reduced entries above.}
\end{center}

\begin{result}[title={The invariant that makes them tractable}]{ink}
Every capture Black can make leaves material that \emph{cannot mate}, so it is
an immediate and permanent draw:
\;$\ldots$K$\times$Q and $\ldots$K$\times$R leave bare kings,
$\ldots$K$\times$B in KBBK leaves KBK,
and in KBNK $\ldots$K$\times$B leaves KNK while $\ldots$K$\times$N leaves KBK.
Consequently no position has a predecessor outside its own table: the unmove
generator needs \textbf{no un-captures at all}. KNK and KNNK join them, and
KNNK is the sharper case, since $\ldots$K$\times$N leaves KNK, in which
\textbf{no mate exists on any board size}.

\medskip
\textbf{This is exactly where it stops,} and knights mark the boundary more
finely than rooks. KRRK looks like KBBK and is not: taking one rook leaves KRK,
which White wins. KNNNK looks like KBBK too and is also not, but only just ---
taking one knight leaves KNNK, which White wins from 1\,448 of $1.9$ billion
positions at $16\times16$. Rare is not never, and \S\ref{sec:knights} is what
that costs. \S\ref{sec:kqkk} then shows ``bare'' is the wrong word, a second
black \emph{king} satisfying every clause above. What breaks the apparatus is
Black being able to \emph{do} something.
\end{result}

