\subsection{Normal chess}\label{sub:kqk-chess}

\subsubsection{Deepest mate}\label{sub:kqk-law}

\begin{result}[title={KQK depth law}]{kqk}
The deepest KQK win on an $n\times n$ board is
\[ \textbf{mate in } \left\lceil \tfrac{3(n-2)}{2} \right\rceil \]
for every $n$ from 9 to 60 computed --- three plies per unit of board size,
exactly what the shrinking-box technique predicts.

\smallskip
\textbf{The ordinary chessboard is the sole exception.} At $n=8$ the formula
gives 9; the true value is 10, attained by a \emph{single} position up to
symmetry.
\end{result}

\subsubsection{Examples}\label{sub:kqk-family}

\noindent
White king in the corner, queen beside it, black king out past the middle:
$\text{wK }a1$, $\text{wQ }b2$,
$\text{bK }(\lfloor n/2\rfloor+2,\ \lfloor n/2\rfloor+1)$, verified deepest for
every $n$ from 9 to 32. The black king's square advances one diagonal step
every two board sizes, the same $\lfloor n/2\rfloor$ that makes the law
alternate between $3n-7$ and $3n-6$.

\medskip
\noindent
\sbpos[coords=false,under={\diag{10}{kqk}},
  intro={$n=10$ --- mate in 12}]{10}{Ka1 Qb2 kh7}\hfill
\sbpos[coords=false,under={\diag{16}{kqk}},
  intro={$n=16$ --- mate in 21}]{16}{Ka1 Qb2 kk10}\hfill
\sbpos[coords=false,under={\diag{22}{kqk}},
  intro={$n=22$ --- mate in 30}]{22}{Ka1 Qb2 kn13}

\medskip
\noindent{\small Dashed, the long diagonal; drawn to a common width, so the
structure is the same picture at every scale. At $n=8$, the law's exception,
the black king sits one square nearer: wK\,$a1$, wQ\,$b2$, bK\,$f5$, unique up
to symmetry.}

\subsubsection{Mating without the table}\label{sub:kqk-rule}

At $n=60$ the KQK table holds 5\,820\,822\,000 entries a side. Is there a
\emph{fixed rule} --- no dependence on $n$, no memory, no lookup --- that mates
from everywhere and is not much slower? There is.

\medskip
\noindent
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.34\textwidth}
\sbpos[width=\linewidth,board=3.11cm,tomove=b,
  under={\cagebox{3}{1}{7}{7}{kqk}\qcut{2}{0}{8}{kqk}},
  intro={the queen's file and rank, and the \textbf{cage} they
  cut out}]{8}{Ka1 Qc1 kd4}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.62\textwidth}\vspace{0pt}\small
If the queen shares neither file nor rank with the black king, her lines cut
the board into four rectangles and he sits in one. To leave it he would have to
step onto a line she attacks every square of, so \textbf{Black cannot enlarge
the cage}: only White can, and only by moving the queen. That asymmetry is the
whole algorithm.

\smallskip
With $R$ the cage and $T$ the \emph{post} the white king walks to,
$\Phi = \bigl(|R|,\ \mathrm{cheb}(\text{wK},T),\ \mathrm{man}(\text{wK},T)\bigr)$
ordered lexicographically is unchanged by every black move, so a policy that
only lowers $\Phi$ cannot cycle. One caveat, checked rather than assumed: the
white king can stand on the queen's line and block it, so every candidate move
is tested against all of Black's replies.
\end{minipage}

\medskip
\noindent
Call a White move \textbf{sound} when, after it, Black cannot capture the
queen, Black has a legal move, the black king is on neither the queen's file
nor her rank, and no black reply either changes the cage or leaves Black with
no move. White plays the first rule that applies.

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}rp{0.82\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{1} & \textbf{Mate}, if a move mates. \\
\textbf{2} & \textbf{Finish}, once width plus height is at most 6: for
$k=1,\dots,5$ in turn, if White has a forced mate in at most $k$ using only
sound moves that never grow the cage, play its first move. \\
\textbf{3} & \textbf{Squeeze}: a sound move that strictly shrinks the cage,
smallest area first and at equal area the squarest. \\
\textbf{4} & \textbf{Walk}: a sound move that keeps the cage and brings the
white king strictly nearer the post. \\
\textbf{5} & \textbf{Wait}: a sound move that keeps the cage, hemming the black
king in most. \\
\textbf{6} & Any legal move that neither hangs the queen nor stalemates. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
Rules 3 and 4 lower $\Phi$, which is bounded below, so neither can repeat a
position. The \emph{iterative deepening} in rule 2 is not decoration: a fixed
depth lets ``mate in at most five'' stay true forever while the pieces shuffle.
Rules 5 and 6 carry no such argument, which is why the rule is scored by
machine; over all 144\,508 white-to-move $8\times8$ positions rule 5 fires 96
times and rule 6 464, against 132\,928 uses of rule 3.

\medskip
\noindent
\textbf{One repair, and it took a $13\times13$ board to find.} With the corner
post alone the rule hangs from 53\,661 positions there: the cage is a single
file eight squares long, so White is winning by every measure, and his king has
reached a post two steps in from the corner where it is useless, the black king
being at the \emph{other} end of the strip. The cure is to send the king where
the work is --- once the strip is seven or more long the post moves beside the
square the queen wants. Both posts are functions of the cage alone, so $\Phi$
survives. All 4\,466\,964 positions up to $12\times12$ pass with the corner
post and say nothing about the defect.

\medskip
\noindent
White's move is fixed to the rule, Black plays anything, and the worst case
comes from backward induction over the induced graph. The ratio to the table's
optimum runs $1.75$, $1.57$, $1.60$, $1.75$, $\mathbf{1.79}$, $1.73$, $1.76$,
$1.72$ over $n=4,6,8,10,11,12,13,14$, with no failure at any size.

\begin{result}[title={A rule that mates from everywhere, at most $1.79\times$ the optimum}]{kqk}
For every board from $4\times4$ to $14\times14$ the rule mates from every legal
position --- 13\,559\,524 in all --- and never takes more than $1.79\times$ as
long as the table. Its worst case grows like $5n/2$ against an optimum near
$3n/2$, so throwing away five billion entries costs a factor tending to $5/3$.

\medskip
\textbf{The control.} Substituting the tablebase's own optimal move for the
rule, in the same harness, reproduces the optimum exactly: ratio $1.00$, zero
failures. That is what says the scoring measures the policy and not the scorer.
\end{result}

\medskip
\noindent
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.44\textwidth}
\begin{stepboard}[width=\linewidth,board=3.44cm,height=49mm,
  intro={wK $a1$, wQ $c1$, bK $d4$ --- mate in 10 with the table, 16 with the
  rule}]{8}{Ka1 Qc1 kd4}
  Qg5, ke4, Qc5, kf4, Qd5, ke3, Qc4, kf3, Qd4, kg3,
  Qe4, kf2, Qd3, kg2, Qe3, kh2, Qf3, kg1, Qe2, kh1,
  Kb2, kg1, Kc2, kh1, Kd2, kg1, Ke3, kh1, Kf3, kg1, Qg2
\end{stepboard}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.52\textwidth}\vspace{0pt}\small
The hardest $8\times8$ position for the rule. Both phases are plain in the cage
size: ten queen moves take it from 35 squares to 3, five king moves walk the
white king in, and the eleventh queen move mates. Note move 1, past the black
king to $g5$ rather than towards it, and that the queen stands still from move
11 to 15.

\smallskip
\textbf{Two clauses that look unnecessary, and are not.} \emph{Never put the
black king on the queen's file or rank}: a check along a line dissolves the
cage, the king being momentarily on both sides of it, while a diagonal check
does not and is often the fastest squeeze there is. \emph{Never let Black reach
a square with no move}: every legal White move must then hand him a flight
square, which usually means opening the cage. Without it the rule loses its way
from 49 positions on $8\times8$; with it, from none.
\end{minipage}

\subsection{Capture the king}\label{sub:kqk-capture}

\subsubsection{Deepest win}\label{sub:kqk-cap-law}

\noindent
\textbf{A convention, once, for every capture table here.} These tables end
when the mover has no chess move --- mate or stalemate, and
\S\ref{sub:cap-onceking} makes both a loss. The solvers of \S\ref{sec:kqkk} and
\S\ref{sec:kings} play on until the king is physically taken, two plies later,
so a depth quoted there is two more than the same depth quoted here.

\smallskip
\noindent
\textbf{The capture rule is worth exactly one move, and not a move more.}
\S\ref{sub:kqk-law}'s closed form carries straight over: chess takes
$2\lceil 3(n-2)/2\rceil-1$ plies and the capture rule
$2\lceil 3(n-2)/2\rceil-3$. The one move is the stalemate dodge --- ordinary
KQK's longest win spends a tempo avoiding a stalemate it is not allowed to
want, and the rule refunds that tempo, once, whatever the board size.

\subsubsection{Examples}\label{sub:kqk-cap-pos}

\medskip
\noindent
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.32\textwidth}
\sbpos[width=\linewidth,board=2.89cm,variant=N,
  intro={chess: K$a1$ Q$b2$ vs k$f5$\\\textbf{19 plies}}]{8}{Ka1 Qb2 kf5}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.32\textwidth}
\sbpos[width=\linewidth,board=2.89cm,variant=C,
  intro={capture: K$a1$ Q$a2$ vs k$e4$\\\textbf{15 plies}}]{8}{Ka1 Qa2 ke4}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.32\textwidth}\vspace{0pt}\small
The deepest position changes as well as the depth. Under the capture rule the
queen starts \emph{beside her own king} rather than on the long diagonal: she
no longer needs room to give the black king a flight square, there being no
reason to give him one.
\end{minipage}

\section{King and rook}\label{sec:krk}

\noindent
The same solver and the same index, with one line of the move generator
changed: a rook is a queen restricted to the four orthogonal rays.

\subsection{Growth}\label{sub:krk-growth}

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=0.82\textwidth, height=28mm,
  xlabel={board size $n$}, ylabel={deepest win (plies)},
  xmin=8, xmax=24, ymin=0, ymax=118, xtick={8,10,...,24},
  grid=both, grid style={ink!12}, tick align=outside,
  axis line style={ink!45}, label style={color=inksoft}, tick label style={color=inksoft},
  legend style={at={(0.02,0.98)},anchor=north west,draw=ink!20,fill=paper,font=\small},
  legend cell align=left,
]
\addplot[krk, thick, mark=*, mark size=1.1] coordinates {
(8,31) (9,35) (10,41) (11,45) (12,49) (13,55) (14,59) (15,65) (16,69) (17,75)
(18,77) (19,83) (20,87) (21,93) (22,97) (23,103) (24,107)};
\addlegendentry{ordinary chess}
\addplot[krk!50, thick, dashed, mark=square*, mark size=1.1] coordinates {
(8,31) (9,33) (10,39) (11,43) (12,47) (13,53) (14,57) (15,63) (16,67) (17,73)
(18,75) (19,81) (20,85) (21,91) (22,95) (23,101) (24,105)};
\addlegendentry{capture rule}
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\noindent
Two plies apart from $9\times9$ up, at $4.76$ plies a board against $4.72$. But
\textbf{at $n=6$, $7$ and $8$ the capture rule saves nothing at all}: $8\times8$
KRK is 31 plies under both games, the opposite of the queen's answer. A rook's
mate is a squeeze along a rank with the kings in opposition, and on a board
that small the squeeze never passes through a position where White would have
preferred a stalemate.

\subsection{Normal chess}\label{sub:krk-chess}

\subsubsection{Deepest mate}\label{sub:krk-law}

\begin{result}[title={KRK depth law}]{krk}
No expression $\lceil (an+b)/c\rceil$ fits, searched exhaustively for $a<60$,
$|b|\le60$, $c\le24$. What holds instead is a recurrence of period six:
\[ m(n) = m(n-6) + 14, \qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad
   m(n) = 14\left\lfloor \tfrac{n}{6} \right\rfloor + c(n \bmod 6),
   \quad c = (-3,0,2,5,7,10) \]
for every $12\le n\le 50$ computed, with \textbf{exactly one} disagreement, at
$n=24$, where the law predicts 53 and the true value is 54.
\end{result}

\subsubsection{Examples}\label{sub:krk-family}

\noindent
All three men on the long diagonal, the black king on the \emph{centre square},
the rook between it and the corner:
\;$\text{wK }a1$, $\text{rook }(k,k)$, $\text{bK }(k{+}1,k{+}1)$,
$k=\lceil n/2\rceil-2$. Verified deepest for every $n$ from 8 to 32, $n=24$
included, where the depth law itself fails.

\medskip
\noindent
\sbpos[coords=false,under={\boxoff{4}{10}{krk}\diag{10}{krk}},
  intro={$n=10$ --- mate in 21}]{10}{Ka1 Rd4 ke5}\hfill
\sbpos[coords=false,under={\boxoff{7}{15}{krk}\diag{15}{krk}},
  intro={$n=15$ --- mate in 33}]{15}{Ka1 Rg7 kh8}\hfill
\sbpos[coords=false,under={\boxoff{9}{20}{krk}\diag{20}{krk}},
  intro={$n=20$ --- mate in 44}]{20}{Ka1 Ri9 kj10}

\medskip
\noindent{\small The tinted quadrant is what the rook's file and rank already
confine Black to. The rook stands next to the \emph{black} king, the opposite
of KQK: a queen confines by radiating from anywhere, a rook by \emph{being} the
fence, so the hardest position is the one where the fence already stands at its
widest useful setting and the white king still has the whole diagonal to
walk.}

\subsection{Capture the king}\label{sub:krk-capture}

\subsubsection{Deepest win}\label{sub:krk-cap-law}

\noindent
Equal depth does not mean the same position. Both maxima below are 31 plies on
$8\times8$, and only the first is the one the textbooks describe.

\subsubsection{Examples}\label{sub:krk-cap-pos}

\medskip
\noindent
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.32\textwidth}
\sbpos[width=\linewidth,board=2.89cm,variant=N,
  intro={chess: K$a1$ R$b2$ vs k$c3$\\all three men in the corner}]{8}{Ka1 Rb2 kc3}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.32\textwidth}
\sbpos[width=\linewidth,board=2.89cm,variant=C,
  intro={capture: K$a1$ R$f2$ vs k$e3$\\the rook thrown across}]{8}{Ka1 Rf2 ke3}
