\noindent
There is no capture-rule line on this chart, and \S\ref{sub:kqkr-capture} says
why.

\subsection{Normal chess}\label{sub:kqkr-chess}

\subsubsection{Deepest mate}\label{sub:kqkr-law}

\noindent
Mate in 35 on the ordinary board is the published figure, not fitted to. What
matters is the rate: between boards two apart the local exponent $\alpha$ for
which $d\propto n^{\alpha}$ runs $1.46$, $1.94$, $2.49$, $2.86$, $4.91$ from
$6{\to}8$ to $13{\to}15$, climbing straight through 2 and onward. A cubic
fitted to $n\le14$ has residuals under a move and predicts 162 at $n=15$,
\[ d(n) \;\approx\; \tfrac{1}{18}n^{3} - \tfrac{1}{7}n^{2}
   - \tfrac{169}{126}n + \tfrac{559}{21}, \]
but it is an interpolant: over so short a range the $n^3$ coefficient is not
identifiable, sliding from $+0.08$ to $-0.02$ as the window moves by one board
with no loss of fit. \textbf{The exponent does not converge to 3. It passes
through it.}

\begin{result}[title={And then the win disappears}]{kqkr}
At $n=15$ the depth overshoots the cubic by more than fifty moves. At $n=16$
the drawn fraction, falling steadily for seven board sizes, jumps from one
position in eight hundred to more than one in four, and the deepest win
\emph{falls}. A whole class has stopped being winnable, and the class is
visible in the depth histogram, which is bimodal at every board size:

\smallskip
\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
$n$ & 10 & 11 & 12 & 13 & 14 & 15 & 16 \\
\midrule
shallow mode (plies) & 21 & 23 & 25 & 27 & 31 & 33 & 35 \\
deep mode (plies)    & 85 & 111 & 141 & 183 & 227 & \textbf{395} & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\smallskip
The shallow mode is untroubled all the way across; the deep one accelerates and
then, at $n=16$, is simply absent. 65 million of the 241 million wins at $n=15$
run deeper than 150 plies, against 1.7 million at $n=16$, and the 110 million
drawn positions are what that mode used to weigh.
\label{sub:kqkr-vanish}
\end{result}

\subsubsection{Examples}\label{sub:kqkr-oneposition}

\noindent
The cleanest evidence is one placement, taken from the drawn list at $n=16$ and
probed on every board that holds it: all four men in the corner, the rook on the
queen's rank between her and the black king.

\medskip
\noindent
\sbpos[width=0.22\textwidth,board=2.22cm,coords=false,
  under={\qcut{6}{0}{8}{kqkr}},over={\ring{3}{0}{kqkr}},
  intro={$n=8$ --- mate in 26}]{8}{Ka1 Qg1 kc1 rd1}\hfill
\sbpos[width=0.30\textwidth,board=3.33cm,coords=false,
  under={\qcut{6}{0}{12}{kqkr}},over={\ring{3}{0}{kqkr}},
  intro={$n=12$ --- mate in 61}]{12}{Ka1 Qg1 kc1 rd1}\hfill
\sbpos[width=0.40\textwidth,board=4.44cm,coords=false,
  under={\qcut{6}{0}{16}{kqkr}},over={\ring{3}{0}{kqkr}},
  intro={$n=16$ --- \textbf{drawn}}]{16}{Ka1 Qg1 kc1 rd1}

\medskip
\noindent
Dashed, the queen's rank and file; ringed, the interposing rook. The position
never changes, only the empty space behind it --- and the extra room is room to
keep interposing.

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
$n$ & 8 & 9 & 10 & 11 & 12 & 13 & 14 & 15 & 16 \\
\midrule
wK\,$a1$, wQ\,$g1$, bK\,$c1$, bR\,$d1$ & 26 & 30 & 37 & 51 & 61 & 77 & 95 &
  \textbf{176} & \textbf{draw} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
This is the shape of a phase transition: a quantity diverging as a parameter
approaches a critical value, and past it the thing it measured is gone.

\subsubsection{What the new draws look like: nothing in the air}\label{sub:kqkr-quiet}

\noindent
A position can be drawn \emph{tactically} --- Black checking, hitting the
queen, a king in contact --- or \emph{structurally}, nothing attacked and White
with no way in. The two are told apart mechanically: call a placement
\textbf{quiet} when nothing whatever is attacked.

\begin{result}[title={Below the transition every draw White faces is tactical; above it most are not}]{kqkr}
\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrr@{}}
\toprule
drawn symmetry classes & $8\times8$ & $15\times15$ & $16\times16$ \\
\midrule
\textbf{White} to move            & 8\,963 & 304\,483 & 110\,091\,285 \\
\quad of them \textbf{quiet}      & \textbf{0} & \textbf{0} & \textbf{76\,718\,643} (69.7\,\%) \\
\addlinespace
Black to move                     & 78\,518 & 4\,573\,792 & 338\,880\,229 \\
\quad of them quiet               & 35\,284 (44.9\,\%) & 3\,145\,919 (68.8\,\%) & 276\,036\,657 (81.5\,\%) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\smallskip
Quiet draws with \emph{Black} to move exist at every board size, so the test is
not vacuous. But with \textbf{White} to move, up to and including $15\times15$,
there is \textbf{not one}: 287\,230 of the 304\,483, $94\,\%$, have the rook
\emph{checking}, and the rest have it hitting the queen, the queen hitting it,
or a king in contact. At $16\times16$ seven of every ten do not.
\end{result}

\medskip
\noindent
\sbpos[width=0.31\textwidth,board=3.44cm,coords=false,tomove=b,
  intro={$15\times15$, \textbf{Black} to move\\wK $a1$ wQ $o2$ bK $c1$ bR $i15$\\
  drawn, but only just: with White to play it is mate in
  21}]{15}{Ka1 Qo2 kc1 ri15}\hfill
\sbpos[width=0.31\textwidth,board=3.44cm,coords=false,
  intro={$16\times16$, \textbf{White} to move\\wK $a1$ wQ $h16$ bK $g2$ bR $j8$\\
  drawn --- and drawn either way}]{16}{Ka1 Qh16 kg2 rj8}\hfill
\sbpos[width=0.31\textwidth,board=3.44cm,coords=false,
  intro={$16\times16$, \textbf{White} to move\\wK $a1$ wQ $b1$ bK $d2$ bR $c3$\\
  drawn either way, all four men in the corner}]{16}{Ka1 Qb1 kd2 rc3}

\medskip
\noindent
None of the three has anything attacked. The $15\times15$ position is drawn
\emph{only} because it is Black's move; the two at $16\times16$ are drawn
whichever side is to move, which cannot happen on any smaller board. At
$15\times15$ Black holds 3.1 million quiet positions, but only \emph{with the
move}.

\subsubsection{What the win is, and what one more file does to it}\label{sub:kqkr-mechanism}

\noindent
On $8\times8$ the KQKR win is \emph{local}: the white king walks to the black
one and zugzwang wins the rook. On $15\times15$ it is not local at all.

\medskip
\noindent
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.40\textwidth}
\begin{stepboard}[width=\linewidth,board=3.66cm,height=52mm,coords=false,
  intro={$15\times15$, White to move --- mate in 197}]{15}{Ka1 Qb1 kd2 rc3}
  Kb2, rc15, Qg6, rc4
\end{stepboard}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.56\textwidth}\vspace{0pt}\small
Black's first move is R$c3$--$c15$: the rook bolts twelve squares up the file,
as far from everything as the board allows, and that is the whole defence. Over
the 383 plies before it falls Black makes \textbf{81 rook moves averaging 5.7
squares}, 28 of them eight or longer. For \textbf{68\,\%} of the line the rook
stands six or more squares from its own king, that distance \emph{growing} as
the game goes on (6.3, 8.1, 10.3, 10.4 by quarter), and for the same $68\,\%$
the black king is within one square of an edge.

\smallskip
So the rook is not sheltering beside its king, where a fork would win it: it is
parked far off on an open line, too distant to be forked with the king and
still able to interpose or check. White's king must cross the whole board, and
his only resource is to take safe squares away from the rook until it runs out.
\textbf{The number of safe squares is what the board size controls.}
\end{minipage}

\begin{result}[title={The same rook moves: every one loses at 15, every one draws at 16}]{kqkr}
After 1.\,K$b2$, Black shuffles the rook along the third rank. Each entry is
the value of the position after that rook move.

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccccccc@{}}
\toprule
rook to & $d3$ & $e3$ & $f3$ & $g3$ & $h3$ & $j3$ & $l3$ & $o3$ & $p3$ \\
\midrule
$15\times15$ & W\,194 & W\,189 & W\,195 & W\,193 & W\,188 & W\,188 & W\,188 &
  W\,188 & --- \\
$16\times16$ & \textbf{draw} & \textbf{draw} & \textbf{draw} & \textbf{draw} &
  \textbf{draw} & \textbf{draw} & \textbf{draw} & \textbf{draw} &
  \textbf{draw} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\smallskip
Note the saturation at $15\times15$: from $h3$ on the value stops moving at
mate in 188, so extra distance buys Black nothing more \emph{there} and White
still grinds it out. One file wider the whole family holds --- not because
Black gains a square ($p3$), but because the scheme becomes tenable, 21 replies
flipping from lost to drawn at once.
\end{result}

\medskip
\noindent
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.46\textwidth}
\sbpos[width=\linewidth,board=4.55cm,coords=false,tomove=b,
  under={\hl{3}{2}{hlmark}\hl{4}{2}{hlmark}\hl{5}{2}{hlmark}\hl{6}{2}{hlmark}%
         \hl{7}{2}{hlmark}\hl{8}{2}{hlmark}\hl{9}{2}{hlmark}\hl{10}{2}{hlmark}%
         \hl{11}{2}{hlmark}\hl{12}{2}{hlmark}\hl{13}{2}{hlmark}\hl{14}{2}{hlmark}%
         \hl{15}{2}{hlmark}},
  intro={$16\times16$, after 1.\,K$b2$: every shaded square holds the draw. On
  $15\times15$ the same rook moves all lose.}]{16}{Kb2 Qb1 kd2 rc3}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.46\textwidth}
\sbpos[width=\linewidth,board=4.55cm,coords=false,
  intro={$16\times16$, the identical shape pushed into the far corner ---
  \textbf{mate in 33}. Take the room away and the win returns.}]{16}{Km13 Qn13 kp14 ro15}
\end{minipage}

\medskip
\noindent
The second board is the control. Slide the four men diagonally without changing
their shape and the value tracks only the open board left behind Black: at
$15\times15$ 197, 198, 177, 149, 100, 29 for shifts $0,4,8,9,10,11$, and at
$16\times16$ drawn for all of those, then \textbf{mate in 33} at shift 12. The
win comes back the moment Black is jammed against $p16$. The operative variable
is the room.

\begin{result}[title={The class that flips is one class, and it can be recognised}]{kqkr}
The set \emph{won but deeper than 150 ply} at $15\times15$ and the set
\emph{drawn} at $16\times16$ agree on every measure taken: 65\,203\,653 against
110\,091\,285 positions, $27.0\,\%$ of the legal ones against $26.6\,\%$,
$69.3\,\%$ quiet against $69.7\,\%$, rook within three of its own king in
$39.5\,\%$ against $36.6\,\%$. Same signature: a compact formation, rook near
its king, board to spare. The deep wins that \emph{survive} at $16\times16$,
only 1\,689\,276, are the mirror image, Black's king already \emph{on} an edge
in $50.7\,\%$ of them. Hence the drawn fraction barely moving to $17\times17$:
the criterion is local, so once there is board enough the share stops
growing.
\end{result}

\noindent
The corner placement's depth on every board that holds it --- 30, 36, 43, 56,
72, 93, 114, \textbf{197}, then \textbf{drawn} for $n=8\ldots16$ --- fits
$d(n)\approx C/(n_c-n)$ with $n_c\approx16.4$ from the closest pair $(14,15)$
and $16.7$--$16.8$ from $(12,15)$ and $(13,15)$.

\begin{result}[title={What this does and does not settle}]{kqkr}
\textbf{Does.} Growth far steeper than quadratic is real, and giving the
defender a piece is what produces it. With a bare black king the confinement
region is described by a handful of numbers of size $O(n)$ and re-forming it
costs $O(n)$, capping the product at $n^2$; a rook that can interpose on the
queen's line breaks that ceiling.

\smallskip
\textbf{Does not.} There is no growth \emph{law} here, cubic or otherwise: the
local exponent rises across the whole measured range instead of settling, so
``cubic'' names the board size at which it passes 3 and nothing more. Whether
any fixed material is cubic \emph{asymptotically} is open, and this suggests
why it may be hard: the defensive freedom that lengthens the win eventually
saves it outright. The transition is bracketed between $n=15$ and $n=16$ and no
more --- not shown to be sharp, nothing beyond $n=17$ computed.
\end{result}

\subsection{Capture the king}\label{sub:kqkr-capture}

\subsubsection{Deepest win}\label{sub:kqkr-cap-law}

\begin{result}[title={The one gap in this document}]{kqkr}
\textbf{There is no capture-rule table for K$+$Q vs K$+$R here.} Every other
material has one; the reason is mechanical. \texttt{src/kqkr.cpp} is a separate
solver with signed entries and a four-way induction, and it does not carry the
\texttt{stalemate-loss} flag the bare-king solver does. Adding it is a real
change to delicate code, and it has not been made.
\end{result}

\noindent
\S\ref{sub:cap-onceking}'s theorem bounds how much could change without any
table being built. Both sides have exactly one king, so the two games differ
\emph{only} where the side to move has no legal chess move: every other value
in \S\ref{sub:kqkr-chess}, the transition at $16\times16$ included, carries
over unchanged. The missing table would answer a narrow question: how many KQKR
stalemates are there, and what does refunding them buy? On the materials that
\emph{were} computed the answer is ``a little, then possibly a lot'' --- the
queen gained one move at every size, the rook nothing below $9\times9$, and two
bishops eventually \emph{lost} twelve plies to new and harder wins. KQKR is the
one endgame here where Black has a piece to be stalemated \emph{with}.

\section{Queen against two kings}\label{sec:kqkk}

\noindent
Black's second man is a king, which puts this material outside ordinary chess.
\S\ref{sub:mating-rules} defines the two \emph{mating} rules and
\S\ref{sec:rules} the capture rule; this section gives the numbers for all
three.

\subsection{Growth}\label{sub:kqkk-graph}

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=0.95\textwidth, height=32mm,
  xlabel={board size $n$}, ylabel={deepest win (moves)},
  xmin=3, xmax=17, ymin=0, ymax=24, xtick={3,4,...,17},
  grid=both, grid style={ink!12}, tick align=outside,
  axis line style={ink!45}, label style={color=inksoft}, tick label style={color=inksoft},
  legend style={at={(0.5,-0.24)},anchor=north,draw=ink!20,fill=paper,font=\small,
                legend columns=2, /tikz/every even column/.append style={column sep=6mm}},
  legend cell align=left,
]
\addplot[kqk, thick, mark=*, mark size=1.1] coordinates {
(3,1) (4,4) (5,6) (6,7) (7,8) (8,10) (9,11) (10,12) (11,14) (12,15) (13,17) (14,18) (15,20) (16,21) (17,23)};
\addlegendentry{KQK --- the queen against \emph{one} black king}
\addplot[kbbk, thick, mark=triangle*, mark size=1.6] coordinates {
(3,4) (4,6) (5,9) (6,10) (7,12) (8,13) (9,15) (10,16) (11,18) (12,19) (13,21)};
\addlegendentry{KQKK, capture rule --- White's deepest win}
\addplot[kqkk, thick, mark=*, mark size=1.1] coordinates {
(3,1) (4,4) (5,4) (6,5) (7,6) (8,7) (9,8) (10,8) (11,9) (12,9) (13,10) (14,11) (15,11) (16,12) (17,13)};
\addlegendentry{KQKK, strict mating rule}
\addplot[kqkk!45, thick, dashed, mark=square*, mark size=1.1] coordinates {
(3,1) (4,7) (5,2) (6,2) (7,2) (8,2) (9,2) (10,2) (11,2) (12,2) (13,2) (14,2) (15,2) (16,2) (17,2)};
\addlegendentry{KQKK, loose mating rule}
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\noindent
Four lines, four different games. The \textbf{strict} rule is the only one
easier than ordinary KQK, at every size from 5 up: two kings that must both be
kept out of check are a liability. The \textbf{loose} rule flattens to mate in
2 from $n=5$ on, its wins a fixed handful of corner cages. The \textbf{capture}
rule is the only one running \emph{above} KQK, White having to win a king and
then mate with what is left --- and it understates that game, being White's
half: Black's deepest win grows from 18 moves at $n=8$ to \textbf{51} at
$n=13$.

\subsection{The mating rules}\label{sub:kqkk-chess}

\subsubsection{Deepest mate}\label{sub:kqkk-strict}

\begin{result}[title={Strict: everything is won, and sooner than against one king}]{kqkk}
\textbf{Every legal white-to-move position is a win}, at every board size from
3 to 17: the white-to-move draw count is exactly zero at each. The $8\times8$
table holds 2\,534\,392 legal white-to-move placements, wins all of them, and
the deepest takes \textbf{7} moves against \textbf{10} for the same queen
against a single king.
\end{result}

\noindent
The second king is a liability because Black must keep \emph{both} out of check
with \emph{one} move, and a queen attacking both at once cannot be answered.
The method is therefore a \textbf{fork} rather than a corner, and a fork has no
use for the edge --- which is the whole of the gap in the chart, driving a king
to the rim costing a distance that grows with $n$ while arranging a fork does
not. Of the 307\,604 mating placements on $8\times8$, only $20.3\,\%$ have both
kings on the rim, and in $\mathbf{56.8\,\%}$ the white king does nothing at
all; on $12\times12$, $78.8\,\%$.

\medskip
\noindent
\textbf{Loose: the same men, and the endgame vanishes.} Let a king stand in
check, forbidding only both at once, and almost nothing is left. On $8\times8$
\textbf{544} of 5\,481\,620 legal white-to-move placements are won, none deeper
than mate in 2 --- White never creates a win, only finishes one the placement
already contains. The board holds \textbf{32} mating placements against
307\,604 under the strict rule; all 32 have both kings on the rim, $87.5\,\%$
side by side, and in every one the white king stands beside a square a black
king would otherwise use. The count is $80n-96$ for $5\le n\le14$ while legal
placements grow like $n^8$.

\begin{result}[title={What the rule costs, and there is no third kind of draw}]{kqkk}
\textbf{93\,508} of the $8\times8$ black-to-move stalemates are no move with
exactly one king in check --- what the ordinary rule would call mate --- out of
94\,624 in all. Only 1\,116 are stalemates in the ordinary sense with neither
king in check. At $16\times16$ the proportion is $99.96\,\%$.

\smallskip
It costs White nothing all the same, never being obliged to walk into one, and
the census says so exactly: $\mathrm{draws} = \mathrm{stalemates} +
\mathrm{queen}\ en\ prise$ at every board size computed --- on $8\times8$,
$94\,624 + 1\,146\,804 = 1\,241\,428$, the entire black-to-move draw count. No
fortress, no positional draw, nothing Black can hold.
\end{result}

\subsubsection{Examples}\label{sub:kqkk-fork}

\medskip
\noindent
\sbpos[width=0.24\textwidth,board=2.44cm,coords=false,tomove=b,
  under={\fray{3}{3}{3}{1}{kqkk}\fray{3}{3}{5}{1}{kqkk}},
  intro={\textbf{\color{kqkk}mate} --- the queen alone}]{8}{Ka1 Qd4 kd2 kf2}\hfill
\sbpos[width=0.24\textwidth,board=2.44cm,coords=false,tomove=b,
  under={\fray{2}{1}{0}{1}{kqkk}\fray{2}{1}{1}{0}{kqkk}},
  intro={\textbf{\color{kqkk}mate} --- the king defends her}]{8}{Kc3 Qc2 ka2 kb1}\hfill
\sbpos[width=0.24\textwidth,board=2.44cm,coords=false,tomove=b,
  under={\fray{1}{0}{2}{0}{hldraw!60!ink}},
  intro={\textbf{not} a mate --- a draw}]{8}{Ka1 Qb1 kc1 kd1}\hfill
\sbpos[width=0.24\textwidth,board=2.44cm,coords=false,tomove=b,
  under={\fray{1}{1}{0}{0}{kqkk}\fray{1}{1}{0}{1}{kqkk}},
  intro={\textbf{\color{kqkk}mate}, loose rules}]{8}{Kc1 Qb2 ka1 ka2}

\medskip
\noindent{\small\textbf{First.} wQ\,$d4$ attacks $d2$ down the file and $f2$
down the diagonal; stepping either king off its ray leaves the other attacked,
and the blocking squares $d3$ and $e3$ are the queen's too. The white king is
in the far corner and contributes nothing. \textbf{Second.} wQ\,$c2$ forks $a2$
and $b1$; beside a black king she must be defended, and wK\,$c3$ does that
while covering $b2$. \textbf{Third.} No legal move, $c1$ in check and $d1$ not
--- one king mated and not the other, which the ordinary rule calls mate and
the strict rule stalemate. \textbf{Fourth.} The loose rule's mate: both kings
on the rim, side by side, the white king covering the square they would use.}

\subsection{Capture the king}\label{sub:kqkk-capture}

\subsubsection{Deepest win}\label{sub:cap-three}

\noindent
The same four men, read three ways. Each rule calls a different set of
placements legal, so the row comparing like with like is the third, which
restricts the capture table to what the strict rule admits.

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
$8\times8$, white to move & placements & White wins & draw & Black wins & deepest \\
\midrule
mating rule, strict          & 2\,534\,392 & 2\,534\,392 (100\,\%) & 0 & --- & 7 moves \\
mating rule, loose           & 5\,481\,620 & 544 (0.01\,\%) & 5\,481\,076 & --- & 2 moves \\
capture rule, quiet only     & 2\,534\,392 & 1\,670\,492 (65.9\,\%) & 863\,852 & 48 & 13 moves \\
capture rule, all placements & 7\,624\,512 & 6\,185\,496 (81.1\,\%) & 1\,419\,192 & 19\,824 & 13 moves \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
Under the mating rules Black is lost everywhere or safe almost everywhere,
depending on the reading of ``both at once''. Under the capture rule he is
neither: he holds a third of the board and he can win.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=0.95\textwidth, height=26mm,
  xlabel={board size $n$}, ylabel={share of quiet placements (\%)},
  xmin=4, xmax=11, ymin=0, ymax=105, xtick={4,5,6,7,8,9,10,11},
  grid=both, grid style={ink!12}, tick align=outside,
  axis line style={ink!45}, label style={color=inksoft}, tick label style={color=inksoft},
  legend style={at={(0.5,-0.30)},anchor=north,draw=ink!20,fill=paper,font=\small,
                legend columns=3, /tikz/every even column/.append style={column sep=5mm}},
  legend cell align=left,
]
\addplot[ink!55, thick, dashed] coordinates {(4,100) (5,100) (6,100) (7,100) (8,100) (9,100) (10,100) (11,100)};
\addlegendentry{mating rule: White wins}
\addplot[kqkk, thick, mark=*, mark size=1.1] coordinates {
(4,19.6) (5,34.5) (6,48.4) (7,58.6) (8,65.9) (9,71.4) (10,75.7) (11,79.1)};
\addlegendentry{capture rule: White wins}
\addplot[kqkr, thick, mark=*, mark size=1.1] coordinates {
(4,79.4) (5,65.5) (6,51.6) (7,41.4) (8,34.1) (9,28.6) (10,24.3) (11,20.9)};
\addlegendentry{capture rule: drawn}
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\noindent
White's share climbs steadily with the board, the opposite of what room usually
does for a defender: the more of it there is, the longer the black kings take
to reach anything and the more the queen has to separate them with. Black's
outright \emph{wins} do not follow --- a fixed handful of corner zugzwangs, 12
at $n=4$ and 64 at $n=11$, against 48 million placements.

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
$n$ & 3 & 4 & 5 & 6 & 7 & 8 & 9 & 10 & 11 \\
\midrule
deepest \textbf{white} win (moves) & 4 & 6 & 9 & 10 & 12 & 13 & 15 & 16 & 18 \\
deepest \textbf{black} win (moves) & 3 & 6 & 10 & 12 & 15 & 18 & 24 & 29 & 36 \\
\midrule
K vs K + K, deepest (plies) & --- & 9 & 17 & 21 & 27 & 33 & 45 & 55 & 69 \\
K + Q vs K, deepest (plies) & --- & 7 & 9 & 11 & 15 & 17 & 21 & 23 & 27 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
The last two rows are the two halves of every win here: White's depth is the
smaller plus the manoeuvring to force a capture, Black's is dominated by the
other, his win being $\ldots$K$\times$Q and then a hunt.

\begin{result}[title={What the rules change about this endgame}]{kqkk}
Four things follow from \S\ref{sec:rules}'s five rules, and not one of them is
an extra clause. A fork wins a king rather than the game. The two black kings
defend each other, so Q$\times$K is answered by K$\times$Q and a capture is
only safe where the white king covers the square. \textbf{White can lose}: a
black king beside his takes it, whatever else stands on the board. And
\textbf{the queen is not expendable} --- $\ldots$K$\times$Q leaves a white king
against two black kings, which on every board a four-man table is built for is
a black \emph{win} rather than the draw it is under every other rule set here.
(Past $14\times14$ it would be a draw; \S\ref{sub:cap-hunt}.)

\smallskip
This rule set breaks all four structural facts of \S\ref{sec:endgames} at once,
with the same four men: Black can check, White can capture, no capture is
drawn, and the table converts three ways rather than none. Hence its own solver
--- signed entries, three sub-endgames in dependency order, and an induction on
buckets keyed by depth rather than in alternation, a conversion being able to
name any depth.
\end{result}

\subsubsection{Examples}\label{sub:cap-deepest}

\noindent
Over the same 2\,534\,392 quiet placements the mating rule wins $100\,\%$ with
White to move; the capture rule wins $65.9\,\%$, draws $34.1\,\%$ and loses 48,
and with Black to move wins $44.9\,\%$ and draws $55.0\,\%$. Black's third of
the board is the phalanx holding: he seldom \emph{wins} from a quiet placement,
he draws by keeping his kings together and his eye on the queen. The 48 are
zugzwangs, and all look like the first diagram.

\medskip
\noindent
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.30\textwidth}
\sbpos[width=\linewidth,board=2.66cm,variant=C,
  intro={White to move, \textbf{lost} in 4 plies}]{8}{Ka1 Qh3 kc1 kc2}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.66\textwidth}\vspace{0pt}\small
Nothing is hanging. Kings on $c1$ and $c2$ cover $a2$, $b1$ and $b2$ between
them and guard each other, so the white king can neither move nor take, and no
queen move stops $\ldots$K--$b2$ and $\ldots$K$\times a1$. \textbf{White has a
queen on the board and is lost because his king is sealed in the corner by two
kings that defend each other} --- a shape with no counterpart in ordinary
chess. The tempo can decide outright: with wK\,$a1$, wQ\,$h1$ against kings
$g1$ and $a8$, White to move wins in 6 and Black to move wins in 18.
\end{minipage}

\medskip
\noindent
\textbf{Both deepest wins, played out to the capture.} Neither ends in a mate:
under these rules there is none.

\medskip
\noindent
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.46\textwidth}
\begin{stepboard}[width=\linewidth,board=3.55cm,height=51mm,variant=C,
  intro={wK $a1$, wQ $a2$ vs kings $c1$ and $e5$ --- \textbf{White wins, 13
  moves}}]{8}{Ka1 Qa2 kc1 ke5}
  Qa2-f2, kc1-d1, Ka1-b2, ke5-e6, Kb2-c3, ke6-e7, Kc3-c4, ke7-e8,
  Qf2-e3, ke8-f8, Qe3-f3, kf8-g8, Qf3xd1, kg8-f7, Kc4-d5, kf7-g7,
  Kd5-e5, kg7-h7, Ke5-f6, kh7-h8, Kf6-g6, kh8-g8, Qd1-d8, kg8-h8, Qd8xh8
\end{stepboard}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.46\textwidth}
\begin{stepboard}[width=\linewidth,board=3.55cm,height=51mm,variant=C,
  intro={wK $a1$, wQ $h1$ vs kings $g1$ and $a8$ --- \textbf{Black wins, 18
  moves}}]{8}{Ka1 Qh1 kg1 ka8}
  kg1xh1, Ka1-b2, kh1-h2, Kb2-c2, kh2-g3, Kc2-d2, kg3-f4, Kd2-d3,
  ka8-b7, Kd3-c4, kb7-c6, Kc4-d4, kc6-d6, Kd4-c3, kd6-c5, Kc3-d3,
  kf4-f3, Kd3-c2, kf3-e3, Kc2-c3, ke3-e2, Kc3-b3, ke2-d3, Kb3-a4,
  kd3-c4, Ka4-a3, kc4-c3, Ka3-a2, kc5-b4, Ka2-a1, kb4-a3, Ka1-b1,
  ka3-b2, Kb1-c1, kb2xc1
\end{stepboard}
\end{minipage}

\medskip
\noindent{\small\textbf{Left, 25 plies.} Six moves of manoeuvring and not one
check --- White is not chasing either king, he is keeping them apart, and only
when the $d1$ king is alone on its side is Q$\times d1$ safe. The second half
is the K\,+\,Q vs K table walking the survivor into the corner. \textbf{Right,
35 plies.} One capture and seventeen moves of pursuit: the far king walks over
from $a8$, moves 5 to 8 forming the pair up, and from there they advance
together, each covering the other. That is the whole reason the queen cannot be
given away.}

\section{Nothing on the board but kings}\label{sec:kings}

\noindent
Delete the queen from \S\ref{sec:rules} and what is left has no pieces in it at
all --- \textbf{three white kings against two black ones} --- the only table
here in which material falls on \emph{both} sides. Either side may take,
neither capture ends anything, and $(w,b)$ becomes $(w,b-1)$ or $(w-1,b)$. Six
tables, built up a lattice rather than a chain:
\[
  (1,1) \;\longrightarrow\; (2,1),\,(1,2) \;\longrightarrow\; (3,1),\,(2,2)
        \;\longrightarrow\; (3,2).
\]
There is no other rule set here: with no piece on the board nothing gives
check, so ``mate'' has no meaning; and rule 4 never fires, a side needing four
men to be stalemated (\S\ref{sub:cap-stalemates}) and none here having three.

\begin{result}[title={Three kings beat two, and it is not close}]{kings}
\textbf{Every white-to-move placement is a win}, on every board computed,
$n=3$ to $n=11$, up to $1\,987\,925\,940$ placements at $11\times11$. Not one
draw. With Black to move the endgame is a White win or a draw and nothing else
from $5\times5$ up.

\smallskip
The reason is one row of the census: \emph{up to $14\times14$, three kings
against one is won with \textbf{either} side to move}. So \textbf{any capture
White can make wins on the spot}, the recapture not mattering, a lone king
being lost against three and against two alike. No mating net, no zugzwang:
White has one problem, getting a king beside a black king on his own move, and
Black has one defence.
\end{result}

\subsection{Growth}\label{sub:kings-growth}

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=0.92\textwidth, height=36mm,
  xlabel={board size $n$}, ylabel={deepest win (plies)},
  xmin=3, xmax=20, ymin=0, ymax=132, xtick={3,4,...,20},
  grid=both, grid style={ink!12}, tick align=outside,
  axis line style={ink!45}, label style={color=inksoft}, tick label style={color=inksoft},
  legend style={at={(0.5,-0.22)},anchor=north,draw=ink!20,fill=paper,font=\small,
                legend columns=2, /tikz/every even column/.append style={column sep=5mm}},
  legend cell align=left,
]
\addplot[kings, very thick, mark=*, mark size=1.3] coordinates {
(3,7) (4,13) (5,29) (6,39) (7,49) (8,63) (9,83) (10,101) (11,125)};
\addlegendentry{$K\!+\!K\!+\!K$ vs $K\!+\!K$ --- the endgame}
\addplot[kings!55, thick, dashed, mark=triangle*, mark size=1.5] coordinates {
(3,3) (4,9) (5,13) (6,17) (7,23) (8,31) (9,37) (10,47) (11,55) (12,65) (13,73) (14,81) (15,91) (16,103) (17,119)};
\addlegendentry{$K\!+\!K\!+\!K$ vs $K$ --- what White converts into}
\addplot[kqkk, thick, mark=square*, mark size=1.1] coordinates {
(3,3) (4,9) (5,17) (6,21) (7,27) (8,33) (9,45) (10,55) (11,69) (12,81) (13,99) (14,123) (15,73) (16,73) (17,73) (18,73) (19,73) (20,73)};
\addlegendentry{$K\!+\!K$ vs $K$ --- the phalanx of \S\ref{sub:cap-hunt}}
\addplot[inksoft, thick, dotted, mark=diamond*, mark size=1.5] coordinates {
