\addlegendentry{$K\!+\!K$ vs $K\!+\!K$ --- what Black converts into}
\addplot[kqkk!55, only marks, mark=o, mark size=2.6] coordinates {
(4,9) (6,21) (8,33) (10,55) (12,81) (14,123) (16,73) (18,73) (20,73)};
\addlegendentry{$K$ vs $K\!+\!K$ --- the same curve, colours swapped}
\addplot[ink!50, thick, dash pattern=on 1pt off 2pt] coordinates {
(3,1) (20,1)};
\addlegendentry{$K$ vs $K$ --- one ply, on every board}
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\noindent
\textbf{$K$ vs $K$ never grows at all}: a bare king cannot be forced anywhere,
so the position is won exactly when the two kings already touch, and then
whoever is to move takes. \textbf{$K$ vs $K\!+\!K$ is $K\!+\!K$ vs $K$ with the
colours exchanged}, and the two tables agree to the ply at all eighteen board
sizes, transition included; they are built independently and from different
seeds, so the open circles lying on the line are a check rather than a
definition. That leaves four distinct curves.

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
$n$ & 8 & 9 & 10 & 11 & 12 & 13 & \textbf{14} & 15 & 16 & 17\\
\midrule
$K\!+\!K$ vs $K$        & 33 & 45 & 55 & 69 & 81 & 99 & \textbf{123} & 73 & 73 & 73\\
$K\!+\!K$ vs $K\!+\!K$  & 35 & 47 & 57 & 71 & 83 & 101 & \textbf{125} & 73 & 73 & 73\\
$K\!+\!K\!+\!K$ vs $K$  & 31 & 37 & 47 & 55 & 65 & 73 & 81 & 91 & 103 & 119\\
$K\!+\!K\!+\!K$ vs $K\!+\!K$ & 63 & 83 & 101 & \textbf{125} & --- & --- & --- & --- & --- & ---\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\subsection{Capture the king}\label{sub:kings-capture}

\subsubsection{Deepest win}\label{sub:kings-rates}

\noindent
The four grow at $14.4$, $14.4$, $9.5$ and $20.4$ plies a board, fitted from
$8\times8$ to the last board each is won on, but none of them is linear and the
slopes say little. \S\ref{sub:kqkr-law}'s measure is the useful one: the local
exponent $\alpha$ for which $d\propto n^{\alpha}$, taken between boards two
apart so the odd--even wobble cancels.

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
$n \to n+2$ & $8$ & $9$ & $10$ & $11$ & $12$ & $13$ & $14$ & $15$\\
\midrule
$K\!+\!K$ vs $K$             & 2.29 & 2.13 & 2.12 & 2.16 & \textbf{2.71} & --- & --- & ---\\
$K\!+\!K$ vs $K\!+\!K$       & 2.19 & 2.06 & 2.06 & 2.11 & \textbf{2.66} & --- & --- & ---\\
$K\!+\!K\!+\!K$ vs $K$       & 1.86 & 1.98 & 1.78 & 1.69 & 1.43 & 1.54 & 1.80 & \textbf{2.14}\\
$K\!+\!K\!+\!K$ vs $K\!+\!K$ & 2.12 & 2.04 & --- & --- & --- & --- & --- & ---\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
\textbf{This corrects the obvious reading of the picture.} The five-man line's
steps grow, $+10$, $+10$, $+14$, $+20$, $+18$, $+24$ from $5\times5$ to
$11\times11$, and a step that doubles looks like a runaway. It is not: a steady
$\alpha=2$ produces linearly growing steps by itself. What preceded the
collapse of $K\!+\!K$ vs $K$ was not its five-board plateau but the
\textbf{single jump to $2.71$ in the last window before it went}, and
$K\!+\!K$ vs $K\!+\!K$ did the same. The five-man endgame shows no sign of it
at $11\times11$, and is quadratic with constant 1: $d/n^2$ runs $1.000$,
$0.984$, $1.025$, $1.010$, $1.033$ at $n=7\ldots11$, a cubic term comes out
$-0.0000$ and changes sign as the window slides, and the ratio to $K\!+\!K$ vs
$K$ runs $1.909$, $1.844$, $1.836$, $1.812$ at $n=8\ldots11$ where a cubic over
a quadratic would grow like $n$. It
will not extrapolate: a power law over the last four boards gives $2.125$, the
plateau $K\!+\!K$ vs $K$ sat on before it jumped.

\textbf{Three kings against one is the outlier, the only curve that bends
twice.} Its exponent \emph{falls} from $1.98$ to $1.43$, then climbs back to
$2.14$, the turn starting at the $13\to15$ window --- exactly where the endgame
underneath it broke. Three kings do not stop beating one at $15\times15$; they
begin paying for the recapture Black has just gained
(\S\ref{sub:kings-break}).

\subsubsection{Examples}\label{sub:kings-ident}

\noindent
\textbf{Two kings against two is exactly two plies worse than two against one},
at every board from $5\times5$ to $14\times14$: $19/17$, $23/21$, $29/27$,
$35/33$, $47/45$, $57/55$, $71/69$, $83/81$, $101/99$, $125/123$. The first two
diagrams show why --- a black king left \emph{en prise} beside a white one with
its partner as far off as the board allows, so White takes for nothing, Black
replies, and what remains is the deepest $K\!+\!K$ vs $K$ hunt there is.

\medskip
\noindent
\begin{stepboard}[width=0.30\textwidth,board=2.89cm,height=46mm,variant=C,
  intro={$8\times8$, \textbf{35 plies}: 1.\,K$c1\times d1$, then 33}]{8}{Ka1 Kc1 kd1 kb3}
  Kc1xd1
\end{stepboard}\hfill
\begin{stepboard}[width=0.30\textwidth,board=2.89cm,height=46mm,variant=C,coords=false,
  intro={$14\times14$, \textbf{125 plies}: 1.\,K$m1\times n1$, then 123}]{14}{Ka1 Km1 kd1 kn1}
  Km1xn1
\end{stepboard}\hfill
\begin{stepboard}[width=0.30\textwidth,board=2.89cm,height=46mm,variant=C,coords=false,
  intro={$15\times15$, \textbf{73 plies}: 1.\,K$e5\times f6$, then only 72}]{15}{Kf4 Ke5 kb1 kf6}
  Ke5xf6
\end{stepboard}

\medskip
\noindent
\textbf{And the identity breaks exactly where that mechanism does.} The third
board looks like the other two, but both endgames stand at 73 plies at
$15\times15$: the identity would need the hunt that follows to be worth 74 with
Black to move, and it is worth \textbf{72}. The longest hunts are no longer
reachable with a spare king hanging beside them.

\smallskip
\noindent
$K\!+\!K$ vs $K\!+\!K$ dies at the same board size and far more completely ---
$13.3\,\%$ won at $14\times14$ to \textbf{0.03\,\%} at $15\times15$, against
$100\,\%$ to $7.0\,\%$ --- White needing two things to go right and Black one.
What survives shrinks rather than grows, 203\,232, 198\,748, 195\,816 won
placements at $n=15,16,17$: corner traps with the board getting bigger around
them. \textbf{Three kings catch a lone king faster than two}, from $5\times5$
on, and the gap widens without bound, 81 against 123 at $n=14$. A third king is
less a third pursuer than a third wall.

\medskip
\noindent
\textbf{A coincidence, recorded rather than explained.} The three-man plus
four-man depth misses the five-man depth by exactly one at every board from
$5\times5$ to $11\times11$ --- 30/29, 38/39, 50/49, 64/63, 82/83, 102/101,
$\mathbf{124}$/$\mathbf{125}$ --- the last a genuine prediction. It is not a
law ($4\times4$ gives 18 against 13), and the play does not support the obvious
reading: were the win \emph{force a capture, then finish}, the first capture
would fall where the summands meet, and instead it comes at ply 5, 8, 17, 27,
26, 43 for $n=3\ldots8$ against depths 7, 13, 29, 39, 49, 63. What runs away
with the board is the \emph{herding}.

\medskip
\noindent
\textbf{The hardest position is White's own men in his own way.} As with the
knights of \S\ref{sub:kn-hardest}: at every board from $8\times8$ to
$17\times17$ the deepest $K\!+\!K\!+\!K$ vs $K$ placement is \textbf{K$a1$
K$b1$ K$c1$} against a black king a couple of files away.

\medskip
\noindent
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.34\textwidth}
\begin{stepboard}[width=\linewidth,board=3.11cm,height=48mm,variant=C,coords=false,
  intro={$10\times10$, White to move, \textbf{47 plies}}]{10}{Ka1 Kb1 Kc1 ke1}
  Ka1-b2, ke1-f1, Kb1-c2, kf1-g1, Kb2-c3, kg1-h2
\end{stepboard}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.62\textwidth}\vspace{0pt}\small
The first six moves are pure deployment: White's three kings begin jammed on
three squares of one edge, each blocking the others, and his difficulty is not
finding the black king. The line runs on to $24.\,\text{K}i9\times j10$, the
black king taken in the far corner 71 squares from where he started. That the
deepest position of a completely won endgame should be one where the winning
side is in its own way is a joke the tables keep making.
\end{minipage}

\subsubsection{Two kings hunt down a lone king, until the board is too big}\label{sub:cap-hunt}

\noindent
The load-bearing row of the census, computed rather than assumed. Black wins
from \textbf{every one} of the 124\,992 placements of K vs K\,+\,K on
$8\times8$, at most 33 plies deep, and from every placement on every board up
to $14\times14$. The method is the point: the two kings stay \emph{side by
side}, so a white king taking one is taken by the other, and in that formation
they walk a lone king to the edge. That is why $\ldots$K$\times$Q ends the
game. The other conversion runs the same way for White: \textbf{K\,+\,Q vs K}
is won from every position with the move, in 17 plies against 19 for KQK.

\medskip
\noindent
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.36\textwidth}
\begin{stepboard}[width=\linewidth,board=3.44cm,height=51mm,variant=C,
  intro={\textbf{K vs K+K}, Black to move --- the white king falls in 17
  moves}]{8}{Kc1 ka1 ke1}
  ka1-a2, Kc1-c2, ke1-e2, Kc2-c3, ke2-e3, Kc3-c4, ka2-b2, Kc4-d5,
  kb2-c3, Kd5-e5, kc3-d4, Ke5-f5, kd4-d5, Kf5-f6, ke3-f4, Kf6-f7,
  kf4-f5, Kf7-e7, kf5-e6, Ke7-f8, kd5-e5, Kf8-e8, ke5-d6, Ke8-f8,
  ke6-f6, Kf8-g8, kd6-e6, Kg8-h8, ke6-f7, Kh8-h7, kf7-g7, Kh7-h8, kg7xh8
\end{stepboard}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.60\textwidth}\vspace{0pt}\small
The $8\times8$ win in full, 33 plies. The value falls by exactly one at every
ply --- $+33$, $-32$, $+31$, $\ldots$, $+1$ --- the certificate that neither
side has anything better.

\smallskip
The kings advance \emph{abreast}, never more than a square apart, so the white
king can never take one without being taken by the other: that mutual guard,
not the number of men, does the work. The last two plies are the point of the
whole rule set --- $16\ldots$K$f7$--$g7$ puts a black king \emph{beside} the
white one, which no mating rule here would allow, and White cannot take it
because the second king covers $g7$. He has only $h8$.
\end{minipage}

\begin{result}[title={The hunt has a critical board size, and it is between 14 and 15}]{kqkk}
Up to $14\times14$ two kings win from \textbf{every} placement with the move,
and the depth climbs faster than linearly the whole way: 17, 28, 41, 50,
\textbf{62} moves at $n=8,10,12,13,14$. At $15\times15$ it collapses.

\smallskip
\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
$n$ & 12 & 13 & \textbf{14} & \textbf{15} & 16 & 18 & 20 & 22 \\
\midrule
Black wins, black to move & 100\,\% & 100\,\% & \textbf{100\,\%} & \textbf{7.0\,\%}
  & 6.0\,\% & 4.7\,\% & 3.8\,\% & 3.1\,\% \\
deepest win (moves)       & 41 & 50 & \textbf{62} & \textbf{37} & 37 & 37 & 37 & 37 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\smallskip
Seven per cent of placements are still won, and the deepest of them is 37
moves --- a number that does not move again: \textbf{exactly 73 plies at every
board size from 15 to 22}. Their count grows like $n^4$ against $n^6$
placements, so the share decays like $n^{-2}$.

\smallskip
What survives is \emph{not} a cornered white king, the first guess: at $n=16$
his distance to the nearest edge is distributed almost identically in the two
sets. Nor is it a phalanx already formed, the black kings standing adjacent in
$2.5\,\%$ of wins and $2.9\,\%$ of draws. What separates them is plain
proximity: the further black king is within four squares of the white one in
$24\,\%$ of won placements and $5\,\%$ of drawn.
\label{sub:cap-hunt-length}
\end{result}

\noindent
So the superlinear climb to $n=14$ was not a growth law but the approach to a
transition, sharper than KQKR's: there the drawn fraction went from $0.13\,\%$
to $26.6\,\%$, here from nothing to $93\,\%$. Both halves are computed twice,
by the symmetry-reduced table and by an unreduced solver sharing no code with
it, agreeing at $n=8$ and $n=12\ldots20$.

\medskip
\noindent
\textbf{One placement, watched across the transition.} The three squares exist
on every board from $6\times6$ up, so the only thing changing is the room
behind the black king.

\medskip
\noindent
\sbpos[width=0.30\textwidth,board=2.89cm,variant=C,
  intro={$8\times8$: \textbf{White wins}, 31 plies}]{8}{Ka1 Kb1 kd4}\hfill
\sbpos[width=0.30\textwidth,board=2.89cm,variant=C,coords=false,
  intro={$14\times14$: \textbf{White wins}, 109 plies}]{14}{Ka1 Kb1 kd4}\hfill
\sbpos[width=0.30\textwidth,board=2.89cm,variant=C,coords=false,
  intro={$15\times15$: \textbf{drawn}}]{15}{Ka1 Kb1 kd4}

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrrrrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
$n$ & 6 & 7 & 8 & 9 & 10 & 11 & 12 & 13 & \textbf{14} & \textbf{15} & 16 & 17\\
\midrule
K$a1$ K$b1$ vs k$d4$ & 17 & 25 & 31 & 43 & 49 & 61 & 71 & 89 & \textbf{109} & \textbf{draw} & draw & draw\\
K$f6$ K$g6$ vs k$b1$ & 17 & 23 & 25 & 29 & 33 & 35 & 43 & 49 & 55 & \textbf{73} & 73 & 73\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
Two placements, two fates: the first runs away, 17 plies to 109, then stops
being a win at all; the second climbs gently and locks at 73 for ever. Not the
corner, since both kings end up in one --- the second is \emph{already caught}
and the first has to be fetched.

\begin{result}[title={Three quarters of the win evaporates}]{kqkk}
The $14\times14$ win spends its 109 plies driving the black king from $d4$ east
along the fourth rank, up the east side and west along the top, where he falls
on move 55. Ask the $15\times15$ table what each position in that drive is
worth: \textbf{the first 81 plies are drawn}, and the line becomes a win only
at ply 82, Black by then pinned in the corner. So the endgame has two phases
and only one depends on the board --- the finish is local and works at $n=15$,
16, 17 alike; the drive needs a small board.
\label{sub:cap-hunt-reach}
\end{result}

\noindent
\textbf{Black is not saved by the new ground.} Where he is pinned on the top
rank of the $14\times14$ win, white kings $i11$ and $j11$, black king $j14$,
his five legal squares \emph{all lose}; on $15\times15$ those same five are
\emph{all drawn}, and of the three squares the extra rank gives him, $j15$
loses. He is saved because the old ground has stopped being a wall: $j14$ is
the edge on one board and an ordinary square on the other.

\medskip
\noindent
\textbf{The hunt has a reach, and it is 6.} White-to-move placements by the
distance from the black king to the nearer white king:

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
separation & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 & 6 & 7 & 8 & $\ge 9$\\
\midrule
$14\times14$, won   & 267\,852 & 449\,696 & 541\,092 & 552\,000 & 502\,780 & 420\,192 & 333\,396 & 253\,872 & \emph{all}\\
$15\times15$, won   & 356\,888 & 16\,000 & 11\,192 & 5\,820 & 2\,328 & 520 & \textbf{0} & \textbf{0} & \textbf{0}\\
$16\times16$, won   & 466\,380 & 15\,300 & 10\,848 & 5\,776 & 2\,328 & 520 & \textbf{0} & \textbf{0} & \textbf{0}\\
$17\times17$, won   & 599\,232 & 14\,796 & 10\,576 & 5\,728 & 2\,328 & 520 & \textbf{0} & \textbf{0} & \textbf{0}\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
At $14\times14$ the reach is the whole board, out to 13, with not one drawn
placement; at $15\times15$ it collapses to \textbf{six}, and no won placement
there or above has the black king seven or more from both white kings. What is
left is a fixed set rather than a shrinking share of a growing one:
\textbf{2328} at separation 5 and \textbf{520} at 6 on all three boards, the
same to the unit. Reach 6 is necessary and not sufficient
(K$a1$ K$b1$ vs k$d4$ has separation 3 and is drawn), but past 6 the question
is closed.

\begin{result}[title={Area is irrelevant. The shorter side is everything}]{kqkk}
$K\!+\!K$ vs $K$ solved on rectangular $n_f\times n_r$ boards by a separate
program --- no symmetry, no index, value iteration to a fixpoint --- checked
against the tables above on every square board from $5\times5$ to $14\times14$,
position, depth and census alike:

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrl@{}}
\toprule
board & squares & shorter side & \multicolumn{1}{c}{$\lfloor(s-1)/2\rfloor$} & won & deepest\\
\midrule
$13\times13$ & 169 & 13 & 6 & \textbf{100\,\%} & 99\\
$14\times14$ & 196 & 14 & 6 & \textbf{100\,\%} & 123\\
$15\times14$ & 210 & 14 & 6 & \textbf{100\,\%} & 141\\
$18\times14$ & 252 & 14 & 6 & \textbf{100\,\%} & 197\\
$24\times14$ & 336 & 14 & 6 & \textbf{100\,\%} & \textbf{299}\\
$20\times13$ & 260 & 13 & 6 & \textbf{100\,\%} & 183\\
\midrule
$15\times15$ & 225 & 15 & \textbf{7} & 6.99\,\% & 73\\
$20\times15$ & 300 & 15 & \textbf{7} & 5.10\,\% & 73\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

A $24\times14$ board has 336 squares, half as many again as $15\times15$, and
\textbf{every placement on it is won}, in up to 299 plies. A $15\times15$ board
has 225 and nine in ten of them are drawn. Size does not decide this.
\label{sub:cap-hunt-why}
\end{result}

\noindent
\textbf{The rule is the shorter side, and the threshold is 14.} Every board
whose shorter side is at most 14 is won from every placement; every board whose
shorter side is 15 collapses to the same local traps and the same frozen 73
plies, $20\times15$'s deepest survivor being the $15\times15$ position exactly.
The long side may be anything. Read as a distance it answers itself: on a board
whose shorter side is $s$ the furthest the black king can get from an edge is
$\lfloor(s-1)/2\rfloor$, which is \textbf{6} for $s=13$ \emph{and} $s=14$ and
\textbf{7} at $s=15$. Two kings can work on a king at most six from an edge.
Whether this 6 is the reach's 6 is not shown.

\smallskip
\noindent
The last column also disposes of an argument used earlier, that steeply
climbing depth signals a transition ahead: on $24\times14$ the depth is 299
plies and still climbing with every file added, with no transition ahead.

\subsubsection{Captures decide it}\label{sub:kings-tempo}

\noindent
On $3\times3$ and $4\times4$ the extra king is worth nothing unless you also
have the move. They are the only boards on which Black wins anything, and on
both the same placements are a White win with White to play and a \emph{Black}
win with Black to play.

\medskip
\noindent
\begin{stepboard}[width=0.30\textwidth,board=2.44cm,height=42mm,variant=C,
  intro={\textbf{White to move: White wins in 3 plies}}]{3}{Ka1 Kc1 Ka3 kb1 kc3}
  Ka1xb1, kc3-b3, Ka3xb3
\end{stepboard}\hfill
\begin{stepboard}[width=0.30\textwidth,board=2.44cm,height=42mm,variant=C,
  intro={\textbf{Black to move: BLACK wins in 5 plies}}]{3}{Ka1 Kc1 Ka3 kb1 kc3}
  kb1xa1, Kc1-c2, kc3xc2, Ka3-b3, kc2xb3
\end{stepboard}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.34\textwidth}\vspace{0pt}\small
The same five men on the same nine squares, and the move is the whole game.
Twelve placements on $3\times3$ behave this way and \textbf{404} on $4\times4$,
where K$a1$ K$d1$ K$a3$ vs k$b1$ k$d3$ is a win in 9 with the move and a
\emph{Black} win in 17 without, the deepest Black win here. From $5\times5$ up
Black never wins again, and the move is never worth \emph{less} than nothing to
either side, so \textbf{this endgame contains no zugzwang at all}.
\S\ref{sub:kn-claim} says the same of two knights, for another reason.
\end{minipage}

\medskip
\noindent
Every quiet black move hands White a white-to-move placement, and those are all
won, so Black is drawn \emph{precisely} when he has a capture leading to a
drawn $K\!+\!K$ vs $K\!+\!K$. That is the entire defence, and it is sharp: the
$6\times6$ placement below gives him ten legal moves, of which \textbf{three}
take a white king and only one draws.

\medskip
\noindent
\begin{stepboard}[width=0.30\textwidth,board=2.44cm,height=42mm,variant=C,
  intro={\textbf{1\ldots\,k$a2\times a1$ draws:} the bare kings stand on $a1$
  and $c1$, two apart}]{6}{Ka1 Kb1 Kc1 kd1 ka2}
  ka2xa1, Kb1xa1, kd1xc1
\end{stepboard}\hfill
\begin{stepboard}[width=0.30\textwidth,board=2.44cm,height=42mm,variant=C,
  intro={\textbf{1\ldots\,k$a2\times b1$ loses in 19:} they stand on $b1$ and
  $c1$, and touch}]{6}{Ka1 Kb1 Kc1 kd1 ka2}
  ka2xb1, Ka1xb1, kd1xc1, Kb1xc1
\end{stepboard}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.34\textwidth}\vspace{0pt}\small
One square's difference in which king Black takes, and the same trades run off
the board into two bare kings that touch or two that do not --- and $K$ vs $K$
is drawn \emph{unless} they already stand beside each other. The king it is
right to take is the one buried in the corner, which looks least worth having.
The share of black-to-move placements this saves falls with the board, $51.2$
to $29.8$ per cent from $5\times5$ to $10\times10$.
\end{minipage}

\medskip
\noindent
\textbf{White's trades are the converse.} Since any capture wins on the spot an
even exchange is not even: it takes 3 vs 2 down to 2 vs 1, which is won. So in
the longest win on the board White walks into the trade on purpose.

\medskip
\noindent
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.34\textwidth}
\begin{stepboard}[width=\linewidth,board=2.89cm,height=46mm,variant=C,
  intro={$6\times6$, White to move, wins in 13}]{6}{Kd3 Kd4 Kb6 ke6 kd5}
  Kd4xd5, ke6xd5, Kb6-b5, kd5-e5, Kb5-c5, ke5-f6, Kd3-e4,
  kf6-e6, Ke4-f4, ke6-f6, Kc5-d5, kf6-e6, Kd5xe6
\end{stepboard}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.62\textwidth}\vspace{0pt}\small
The $6\times6$ deepest win --- K$a1$ K$f1$ K$a6$ vs k$d2$ k$a4$, 39 plies ---
caught at ply 27, after thirteen moves of herding. Two plies, no change in
material, and the endgame is over: three against two has become two against
one, won from every placement there is (\S\ref{sub:cap-hunt}). With kings only
a trade changes not the \emph{balance} but which endgame you are in.

\smallskip
\textbf{And White can never manufacture the first capture.} Call a placement
\emph{quiet} when no white king stands beside a black one. From $5\times5$ to
$12\times12$, either side to move, White wins \emph{not one} --- 84.2 million
of them at $12\times12$ and the count is zero. So \textbf{every white win
begins with something already hanging}, and this material is a race for the
first capture, decided before either side moves.
\end{minipage}

\medskip
\noindent
That also disposes of the mirror defence: Black \emph{can} answer every white
move with its image, but from a quiet position he never has to. The only
\emph{symmetric} quiet win is on $4\times4$, K$a1$ K$d4$ against k$d1$ k$a4$,
13 plies for whoever moves; the same idea on $3\times3$ flips sign, whoever
moves \emph{losing} in 4, and on $5\times5$ it is a dead draw.

\medskip
\noindent
\textbf{Raise the board by one file and it all evaporates.} Not because Black
keeps out of the way --- from a quiet position he usually \emph{cannot} retreat
to another --- but because he does not need to. Two black kings side by side
defend each other, so a white king that takes one is recaptured, leaving $K$ vs
$K$. Linking them is nearly sufficient on its own: with the kings adjacent
White's share over \emph{all} placements runs $21.5$ down to $1.0$ per cent at
$n=4\ldots9$, against $34.8\,\%$ unlinked on $8\times8$, and the survivors are
all the same jam.

\subsubsection{Where it must break}\label{sub:kings-break}

\noindent
Everything above rests on one clause, \emph{any capture White can make wins on
the spot}, and that clause is a statement about $n\le14$. It expires.

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
 & $13$ & $\mathbf{14}$ & $\mathbf{15}$ & $16$ & $17$\\
\midrule
$K\!+\!K$ vs $K$, white to move        & all won & \textbf{all won} & \textbf{7.0\% won} & 6.0\% & 5.3\%\\
$K\!+\!K\!+\!K$ vs $K$, white to move  & all won & all won & \textbf{all won} & all won & all won\\
$K\!+\!K\!+\!K$ vs $K$, black to move  & all won & all won & \textbf{8.9\% drawn} & 8.0\% & 7.2\%\\
$K\!+\!K$ vs $K\!+\!K$, white to move   & 15.1\% won & \textbf{13.3\% won} & \textbf{0.03\% won} & 0.02\% & 0.01\%\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
Two kings stop beating one at $15\times15$. \textbf{Three kings still beat
one} --- all $415\,850\,400$ white-to-move placements at $15\times15$ are won,
and $16\times16$'s and $17\times17$'s too --- but the black-to-move half goes,
and every new draw is the same draw: Black takes a white king and reaches the
$K\!+\!K$ vs $K$ that has just stopped being won. That is precisely the
recapture the five-man win assumed could be ignored.

Black's side of it gets easier at the same moment and by more. His entire
defence is a capture into a \emph{drawn} $K\!+\!K$ vs $K\!+\!K$, and that
endgame's white-to-move drawn share runs $84.9\,\%$, $86.7\,\%$,
\textbf{99.97\,\%} at $n=13,14,15$: up to $14\times14$ he must choose his
capture, past the transition he barely has to look. It costs White time in the
half he still wins --- $K\!+\!K\!+\!K$ vs $K$ runs 31, 37, 47, 55, 65, 73, 81,
91, 103, 119 for $n=8\ldots17$, steps of $+6$ to $+10$ up to $15\times15$ and
then $+12$ and $+16$. The turn begins exactly where the endgame underneath it
broke, as one would expect if these transitions propagate up the lattice.

\begin{result}[title={What is settled here, and what is not}]{kings}
$K\!+\!K\!+\!K$ vs $K\!+\!K$ is a White win from every white-to-move placement
for $3\le n\le11$ --- a computation, not an argument, and it does not extend
past $14\times14$ by the reasoning above, because the reasoning stops being
true there. Whether the endgame survives its own transition is \textbf{not
settled}, and the depth data does not settle it either way: by
\S\ref{sub:kings-rates}'s measure the five-man line is flat at
$\alpha\approx2.05$ and shows none of the last-window jump both collapsing
endgames made, but the clause the win rests on is false at $15\times15$
whatever the depth is doing at $11\times11$. What would settle it is out of
reach by four board sizes --- the $15\times15$ table is $5.9\times10^{9}$
canonical slots per side to move, 24\,GB at two bytes an entry, against the
2.4\,GB that $12\times12$ costs.
\end{result}

\section{Capture-the-king chess, and the other rule sets}\label{sec:rules}

\noindent
In chess the objective is to \emph{capture} the king, and mate is the name for
a capture that cannot be prevented. Taking that literally is the second rule
set every section above is measured under.

\begin{result}[title={The rules: every king is a man, and you win by taking them all}]{kqkk}
Let each side have $k\ge1$ kings. Everything else is chess.

\smallskip
\begin{enumerate}\setlength{\itemsep}{2pt}
\item Every man moves and captures exactly as in chess, kings included: one
  square in any direction, taking whatever stands on it.
\item \textbf{A king is an ordinary piece in respect of check.} It may move to
  an attacked square, it may be left standing on one, and it may be captured
  like any other man. There is no check, no checkmate and no pin: \emph{every}
  move that obeys rule 1 may be played.
\item A player wins the instant the opponent's \emph{last} king is taken. The
  game ends with that capture and nothing that would have followed it matters
  --- in particular a player may take the last king with a move that leaves
  their own king hanging. Losing a king while others remain costs a piece and
  nothing more.
\item A player who still has a king and no legal move is stalemated and the
  game is drawn. Under rule 2 that requires \emph{total immobility}, which
  \S\ref{sub:cap-stalemates} characterises; for the material in this document
  it cannot happen at all.
\item Promotion and \emph{en passant} are as in chess, promotion being to
  queen, rook, bishop or knight. Castling plays no part.
\end{enumerate}

\smallskip
Nothing here privileges one king over another, or Black's kings over White's.
\end{result}

\subsection{One king a side: chess, up to stalemate}\label{sub:cap-onceking}

\noindent
Rule 2 sounds like a large change and rule 3 like a small one; with one king a
side it is the other way round. Call a position \textbf{sound} when the side
\emph{not} to move does not have its king attacked --- the condition every
chess-reachable position satisfies, and the one every table here uses to decide
which slots are legal.

\begin{result}[title={Theorem. With one king a side, capture rules are chess with one clause changed}]{kqkk}
Let $P$ be a sound position with one king a side, and let $\mathrm{cap}(P)$ and
$\mathrm{chess}(P)$ be its values under the rules above and under the ordinary
rules. Then $\mathrm{cap}(P)=\mathrm{chess}^{\ast}(P)$, where
$\mathrm{chess}^{\ast}$ is ordinary chess with exactly one clause replaced:
\emph{when the side to move has no legal chess move, the game is drawn if that
side has no move under rule~2 either, and otherwise that side loses.}

\smallskip
So the two games agree except where the side to move has no chess move, and
there they differ in two ways and only two: \textbf{stalemate becomes a loss},
the familiar case; and \textbf{a checkmate becomes a draw}, when the mated side
has no move under rule 2 at all. Ordinary checkmate is otherwise still a loss,
and ordinary stalemate with the mover totally immobile is still a draw.
\end{result}

\noindent
\textbf{Proof.} \emph{(i)} In a sound position the side to move does not attack
the enemy king, so it has no king capture available; a king capture can only
follow a move that left the mover's own king attacked. Call such a move
\emph{self-exposing}. The moves that are not self-exposing are exactly chess's
legal moves, and they lead to sound positions again. \emph{(ii)} A
self-exposing move loses at once: some enemy man can move onto the king's
square, rule 2 makes that legal, and rule 3 ends the game there. \emph{(iii)}
So if the mover has a chess move, deleting the self-exposing moves changes
nothing and what remains is the chess move list; if it has none but has some
move, every move loses by (ii); if it has no move at all, rule 4 draws.
Backward induction over the sound positions --- a finite graph, once the same
repetition rule is given to both games --- yields
$\mathrm{cap}=\mathrm{chess}^{\ast}$.\hfill$\square$

\smallskip
\noindent
Hence the corollary that makes the two games feel alike: \textbf{optimal play
never gives a king away from a position that is not already lost.}

\subsubsection{Which stalemates survive}\label{sub:cap-stalemates}

\begin{result}[title={Corollary. The surviving stalemates are exactly the totally immobile positions}]{kqkk}
The side to move is stalemated exactly when \emph{no man of that side has any
move at all}: (i) every non-pawn man, its king included, has every square it
could reach either off the board or occupied by a man of \emph{its own} colour
--- an enemy man there would be capturable, hence not a blocker --- and (ii)
every pawn has the square in front occupied and no enemy man on either capture
square.

\smallskip
Two consequences. \textbf{The immobile side needs at least four men}, since by
(i) every square beside its king holds one of its own and a king has at least
three of those. And \textbf{if the immobile king is attacked, it is attacked by
a knight}: everything beside it is occupied, so a line piece would have to bear
through an occupied square and a pawn or king would have to stand on one.

\smallskip
So they fall into two families, the second being the whole of the theorem's
second bullet: \textbf{(S)} the immobile king unattacked, which both rule sets
draw; \textbf{(M)} the immobile king attacked, necessarily by a knight, which
ordinary chess calls \emph{checkmate} and these rules call a draw. It is the
only case where capture rules are kinder to the defender.
\end{result}

\noindent
Both families are inhabited; the diagrams differ by one white knight.

\medskip
\noindent
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.30\textwidth}
\sbpos[width=\linewidth,board=2.89cm,variant=C,tomove=b,
  intro={\textbf{(S)} black to move, unattacked and with no move at all: a draw
  under both rule sets}]{8}{Kh8 Rd1 ka1 rb1 bc1 pa2 pb2 pd2}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.30\textwidth}
\sbpos[width=\linewidth,board=2.89cm,variant=C,tomove=b,
  intro={\textbf{(M)} the knight checks $a1$: \emph{mate} in ordinary chess,
  stalemate and a draw here}]{8}{Kh8 Rd1 Nc2 ka1 rb1 bc1 pa2 pb2 pd2}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.36\textwidth}\vspace{0pt}\small
Black is immobile in both, man by man: the king shut in by its own rook and
pawns; the rook on $b1$ with $a1$, $b2$, $c1$ all its own; the bishop on the
rim with only $b2$ and $d2$; the pawns blocked, with nothing but their own men
to take, and $d2$ blocked by a rook a pawn cannot capture. In (M) the knight on
$c2$ is untouchable too, and gives the check no line piece could give.
\end{minipage}

\begin{result}[title={None of this can happen to the material in this document}]{kqkk}
Immobility needs four men on the immobile side. Every side of every table here
has two: king and queen, or king and king. So rule 4 never fires, and every
table sees only the first half of the theorem --- the half that turns the
stalemate draws of ordinary chess into wins. That is exactly why
\textbf{K + Q vs K} is won here in 17 plies against 19 in the ordinary game.

\smallskip
\textbf{Checked against the ordinary table.}
\texttt{./kqk kqkk -{}-capture -{}-theorem} walks every K\,+\,Q vs K placement
for $3\le n\le9$ against ordinary KQK, which this program builds with a
different solver. Every chess win stays a win, every loss a loss, and
\textbf{every chess checkmate and stalemate has capture value exactly $-2$}: on
$8\times8$, 364 checkmates and 872 stalemates, with 22\,176 chess draws staying
drawn --- the published KQK census to the man, so three components agree. The
last count says the drift does not propagate.
\end{result}

\subsection{More than one king: the mating rules}\label{sub:mating-rules}

\noindent
\S\ref{sec:kqkr} armed the defender; this does the opposite in the same slot.
Black's second man is a second \emph{king}: it can never attack anything, the
two may stand beside each other and each blocks the queen's rays for the other,
and neither may stand on or beside the white king.

\begin{result}[title={A mate counts only when both kings are mated at once}]{kqkk}
That sentence is the endgame, and it has two readings. They differ in one
predicate, so the program computes both.

\smallskip
\textbf{Strict.} After Black's move neither king may be left attacked --- the
ordinary rule of chess, applied to each king in turn. Mate is both kings
attacked with no move of either escaping. \emph{No move at all, with only one
king attacked, is stalemate} and a draw: what the ordinary rule would call
mate, this one declines to count.

\smallskip
\textbf{Loose.} A black king may stand in check. What Black may not do is leave
\emph{both} kings attacked at once.

\medskip
Everything else survives, which is why this endgame keeps the cheap machinery
of \S\ref{sec:endgames} while KQKR does not; only the \emph{index} changes, the
block key becoming a king \emph{triple}. A third reading drops mate altogether
and plays for the kings themselves: the capture rule above.
\end{result}

\section{How far any of this can be trusted}\label{sec:trust}

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lp{0.66\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Bellman re-derivation} & Every entry recomputed from its successors
with an independent forward move generator: $n\le24$ for KQK and KRK, $\le14$
for KBBK and KBNK, $\le16$ for KQKR, $\le17$ for KQKK, $\le11$ for KQKK under
capture rules, $\le16$ for KNK and KNNK, $\le10$ for KNNNK, $\le8$ for the
kings-only lattice --- there all six tables at once, a five-man entry being
only as good as the entries it reads. Larger boards on a sample. \\[1.5mm]
\textbf{Unreduced reference solver} & The same endgame solved with \emph{no}
symmetry, by a different algorithm, compared position by position: 33\,030\,144
placements at $n=8$ for each bare-king endgame; every $n^8$ \emph{ordered}
placement to $n=6$ for KQKK and its four capture tables, which checks the
king-triple index at placements reachable only through a symmetry; every
ordered placement to $n=7$ for KNNNK, 530\,832\,288 of them; and for
\S\ref{sec:kings} the whole lattice re-solved as a single hash of every state,
testing ranking, $D_4$ reduction and conversion seeding at once. This is what
catches indexing bugs, which a self-consistency check structurally cannot. Both
bugs the capture rules cost were in the \emph{checker}: a solver that sweeps
until nothing changes stops too early on a table that converts, and one that
takes a win as soon as it is computable records the first win rather than the
shortest. \\[1.5mm]
\textbf{Published $8\times8$ figures} & 462 canonical king pairs, 364 KQK
checkmates, 872 stalemates, 22\,176 queen-\emph{en-prise} draws and KQKR's mate
in 35, all reproduced exactly; KRK, KBBK and KBNK then \emph{derived} rather
than looked up. \textbf{KQKK, KNNNK and the kings-only tables have no published
figure to check against.} KNNNK has instead the unreduced solver over all
$8^{10}$ ordered placements at $n\le7$, and the 120 KNNK mates were counted
independently by a throwaway script. \\[1.5mm]
\textbf{Naive generator and index} & For KQKR and KQKK, where brute force runs
out of memory long before the interesting boards. Each move generator is
compared against one that walks every ray a square at a time; KQKR's index
against the definition of a $D_4$ orbit, KQKK's against the requirement that a
value be constant on a symmetry class, exchange of the two black kings
included. Random placements to $n=16$ and $n=12$. \\[1.5mm]
\textbf{A second, independent solver} & $K\!+\!K$ vs $K$ is a table of
\S\ref{sec:kings} and also $K$ vs $K\!+\!K$, which \S\ref{sub:cap-hunt} builds
out of the four-man capture solver: different index, generator and induction.
The two agree on the win count, the deepest win \emph{and} the white-to-move
draw count from $8\times8$ to $20\times20$ --- on the climb, on the collapse,
and on the 73 plies frozen after it. Two solvers sharing no code agreeing on
where a transition sits is the strongest evidence here that it is real. A
\emph{third}, \texttt{tests/rect\_kk.cpp}, reproduces the census on every
square board to $14\times14$ before being pointed at the rectangles of
\S\ref{sub:cap-hunt-why}. \\[1.5mm]
\textbf{A guess not made} & KNNNK's sub-table could have been assumed instead
of built, and the assumption would have been wrong. The solver refuses to run
without a KNNK table attached. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{result}[title={The bug that mattered}]{ink}
KBNK first reported mate in \textbf{35} on $8\times8$. The textbook value is
33, and that discrepancy is what exposed it.

Phase~B treated ``the black king steps onto a white piece'' as a capture, hence
a draw. With one white piece that is sound; with two it is wrong. And it
\emph{hid}: two bishops can only defend each other when same-coloured, and
those positions are drawn anyway, so the bug was live in KBBK for as long as
KBBK existed and changed not one entry. A bishop and knight defend each other
freely, and there it cost 2\,816 lost positions on $5\times5$. The verifier and
the unreduced solver flagged the same positions independently --- the wrong
answer being a plausible number rather than a crash is the argument for keeping
both.
\end{result}

